  "profile": {
    "video_id": "iron-snail:c6blgVapNWY",
    "purpose": "A roundup of seven chore coats from different brands. It covers the history and anatomy of the chore coat, then looks at each coat's fabric, construction, fit and details, and at the brand behind it.",
    "video_type": "comparison",
    "thesis": "There is no single official historical chore coat. Details like cuffs, pockets, length, fabric and weight show what each brand is referencing, so the video takes the chore coat apart through seven examples, from American workwear to French moleskin to modern reinterpretations.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "Chore coats became standardized and generic during the industrial revolution. Details like cuffs, pocket style, length and thickness reveal what a brand is referencing historically.",
      "American chore coats (Carhartt, Levi's, Pointer) favoured duck canvas and denim with triple-stitched seams and rivets. French ones (Le Mont St. Michel) favoured brushed moleskin.",
      "The Pointer coat (made by L.C. King) uses 12 oz Mount Vernon duck canvas, the same as Carhartt's made-in-USA line, and is cut short like a trucker jacket.",
      "Buck Mason makes a P41-style herringbone chore jacket and a felted bouclé knit chore coat, a lighter California take on the style.",
      "Paynter sells in numbered batches of about 800 that sell out in minutes. Its Bill's blue is named after photographer Bill Cunningham.",
      "The video explains moleskin's popularity in Brittany by its mild, windy, rainy climate. Le Mont St. Michel used high-quality long-staple combed cotton.",
      "The Drake's selvedge denim chore coat (about $700) has a double-layer shoulder panel, partial triple stitching, lined and riveted pockets, and an interior cut on the panel.",
      "Chore coats, like denim, are meant to fade and soften with heavy wear."
    ],
    "outline": [
      {
        "title": "Introduction to chore coats",
        "summary": "A cold-open teaser of several coats, including a double-denim coat and a hyped sell-out brand, before the video's goal is set up.",
        "first_segment": 0,
        "last_segment": 1
      },
      {
        "title": "History and anatomy",
        "summary": "Explains that chore coats were standardized during the industrial revolution. Cuffs, pockets, length and thickness show historical references, with Carhartt as an American example (triple stitching, duck canvas).",
        "first_segment": 2,
        "last_segment": 3
      },
      {
        "title": "The French standard",
        "summary": "Introduces Le Mont St. Michel's moleskin coat as the king of chore coats and sets the goal of breaking the chore coat into its parts. Includes a greeting and spring chat.",
        "first_segment": 3,
        "last_segment": 5
      },
      {
        "title": "Pointer chore coat",
        "summary": "The Pointer coat made by L.C. King, a Portlandia-embroidered eBay find in 12 oz Mount Vernon duck canvas. Covers its trucker-like short cut, simple sewing, and the brand's history and possible closure.",
        "first_segment": 5,
        "last_segment": 8
      },
      {
        "title": "Buck Mason P41 jacket",
        "summary": "After a sponsor read, covers Buck Mason's herringbone twill P41 chore jacket, its WWII uniform reference, a midweight dense weave and reinforced stress points.",
        "first_segment": 8,
        "last_segment": 13
      },
      {
        "title": "Buck Mason felted coat",
        "summary": "Buck Mason's felted wool chore coat: a bouclé-yarn knit that is double felted for strength. Presented as a lighter, breathable California take.",
        "first_segment": 13,
        "last_segment": 16
      },
      {
        "title": "Carhartt heritage",
        "summary": "A heavily worn Carhartt chore coat with triple-stitched seams, an oversized fit and a corduroy collar. Covers how chore coats break in, and other US makers like Levi's, Pointer and Big Yank.",
        "first_segment": 16,
        "last_segment": 17
      },
      {
        "title": "Paynter batch jackets",
        "summary": "Paynter's husband-and-wife brand: numbered batch drops of about 800 that sell out fast, corozo nut buttons, limited patches, and the Bill's blue colour named after Bill Cunningham. Also covers why chore coats should fade.",
        "first_segment": 17,
        "last_segment": 20
      },
      {
        "title": "Le Mont St. Michel",
        "summary": "The Breton work jacket in heavyweight moleskin. Covers why moleskin suited Brittany's climate, how moleskin is made, and the brand's use of long-staple combed cotton.",
        "first_segment": 20,
        "last_segment": 25
      },
      {
        "title": "Modern Drake's interpretation",
        "summary": "The Drake's selvedge denim chore coat: lined and riveted pockets, mixed nickel and copper buttons, partial triple stitching, a double-layer shoulder panel and an interior cut. Covers what you pay for with Drake's.",
        "first_segment": 25,
        "last_segment": 29
      }
    ],
